{"title":"1967 IMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_September_2024)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remarks (added by pf02, September 2024)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Note\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Note</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Proposition\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proposition</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Corollary\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Corollary</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Lemma\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Lemma</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-3 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5.3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Prove that if one and only one edge of a tetrahedron is greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />,\nthen its volume is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/2/b9224741fb87a59c377403128e7946d555863895.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\le \\frac{1}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"34\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/8/3783bdc1fded5b7c2932c0a33ef44a6150435104.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"62\" height=\"13\" /> and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/8/598141cec89bb44405e53c44ba75a25f8b5981bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=x$\" width=\"62\" height=\"13\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/f/3efd92d7eef6e4b04b61b6ff7e353462a2607585.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P,Q,R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" /> be the feet of perpendicular from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/5/f75d6d58b03031bd7cb52a7ca886a8ddf369d21c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" /> and from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, respectively.\n</p><p>Suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/6/4e69c5fb72fe2ddb422d77c550f7bb1d95c84af9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BP&gt;PA$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"81\" height=\"13\" />. We have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/3/dc3d172f920e1ef68d74eeda61261053026d86f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CP=\\sqrt{CB^2-BT^2}\\le\\sqrt{1-\\frac{x^2}4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"257\" height=\"43\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/b/a8b0ed42a4d3813d48164e51d7755bb7eded6f9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CQ\\le CP\\le\\sqrt{1-\\frac{x^2}4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"178\" height=\"45\" />. We also have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/8/9f8bfacc8df79e2ea3d0b1261b92485497723331.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DQ^2\\le\\sqrt{1-\\frac{x^2}4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"133\" height=\"43\" />. So the volume of the tetrahedron is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/3/d335627a7e08a495225087788c7106d7ca4560d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac13\\left(\\frac12\\cdot AB\\cdot DR\\right)CQ\\le\\frac{x}6\\left(1-\\frac{x^2}4\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"288\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p><p>We want to prove that this value is at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/0/a505b2973908a972c4a974b76ad7d25e5b616167.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac18$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />, which is equivalent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/9/77911255c56c8d8f760c6257fb6ff683b2910a0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1-x)(3-x-x^2)\\ge0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"184\" height=\"19\" />. This is true because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/3/4e3878ace16f67e1cf4b37c7983a7691bff305e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;x\\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>The above solution was posted and copyrighted by jgnr. The original thread can be found here: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external autonumber\" href=\"https://aops.com/community/p1480514\">[1]</a>\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Remarks_.28added_by_pf02.2C_September_2024.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_September_2024)\">Remarks (added by pf02, September 2024)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Remarks (added by pf02, September 2024)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The solution above is essentially correct, and it is nice, but it is\nso sloppily written that it borders the incomprehensible.  Below I\nwill give an edited version of it for the sake of completeness.\n</p><p>Then, I will give a second solution to the problem.\n</p><p>A few notes which may be of interest.\n</p><p>The condition that one side is greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is not really\nnecessary.  The statement is true even if all sides are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/1/8d1d3e9b91fd51e596ff2f380746034a1d58aa60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"27\" height=\"14\" />.\nWhat we need is that no more than one side is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/8/31892a6fc3ee94697724ac48f6fe39903de594ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$&gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>The upper limit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/3/6136193afb3f1957d9feaa1b626ccc1e01eed6f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" /> for the volume of the tetrahedron\nis actually reached.  This will become clear from both solutions.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Assume that five of the edges are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/1/8d1d3e9b91fd51e596ff2f380746034a1d58aa60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"27\" height=\"14\" />.  Take them to be the\nedges other than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" />.  Denote <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/0/0a0bbad507209d4947c10a4c8ab5274bff49d568.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"65\" height=\"14\" />.  Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/1/b31cab458dc17cdaa49b467c836004d1738cae97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P, Q, R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"56\" height=\"16\" /> be the\nfeet of perpendiculars from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to the plane\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6adfd3ad27a2dddeb0af7131d73c33c86991e93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, and from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, respectively.\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Prob_1967_2_fig1.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/db/Prob_1967_2_fig1.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"404\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>At least one of the segments <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/a/a7a39c5137a8d33a87ec258f46538b1d148979c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AP, PB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"65\" height=\"18\" /> has to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/e/37ea8cb3a558f8c432f943c5633a46e3827382ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ge \\frac{x}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"35\" height=\"34\" />.\nSuppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/d/11dfc82661c018bdc4269ed25eb3efc0c2e3bceb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$PB \\ge \\frac{x}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"69\" height=\"34\" />.  (If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/b/e3bb4eeb347e74e025fd175c22f1f7275af0555e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> were bigger that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/6/876fba774d252c254bd1496f6a44b4db630e55cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{x}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"16\" height=\"34\" />\nthe argument would be the same.)  We have that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/1/b81363d2e1f67f6a6179a3654b241ec993f29fc3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CP = \\sqrt{BC^2 - PB^2} \\le \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{x^2}{4}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"260\" height=\"45\" />.  By the same\nargument in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/5/f75d6d58b03031bd7cb52a7ca886a8ddf369d21c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/e/60ee4e3cb66401f960171e08134bb8f405393aba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DR \\le \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{x^2}{4}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"126\" height=\"45\" />.\nSince <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/2/9d282128ba99e28e541453439328d43a1edfdd38.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CQ \\perp$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" /> plane <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6adfd3ad27a2dddeb0af7131d73c33c86991e93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/d/8ad5a78a8f8b876fb9e743b6ffb2ba56d9d3a29a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CQ \\le CP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"18\" />, so\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/2/4/c24f15cd13085ee71c330bd7ff6b4c3a5ff6a757.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CQ \\le \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{x^2}{4}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"126\" height=\"45\" />.\n</p><p>The volume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/d/12d58aa29201da09d8e620f8698e3a37547f6b4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> of the tetrahedron is\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/1/801ef0651fa8f3f39ba4512284fd899e74646f93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V = \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot ($\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"74\" height=\"38\" />area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/0/d50c8d37c4051ea3643d6d03fa60fd0346424b7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD) \\cdot$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"74\" height=\"20\" />(height from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/b/4/8b4d267c2d9887c34b53860264bc941fde063266.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C) = \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot AB \\cdot DR \\right) \\cdot CQ \\le \\left( \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot x \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{x^2}{4}} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{x^2}{4}} \\right) = \\frac{x}{6} \\left( 1 - \\frac{x^2}{4} \\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"617\" height=\"55\" />.\n</p><p>We need to prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/e/1de5eb6610f89a0a3df0a02c4594b5678c546e62.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{x}{6} \\left( 1 - \\frac{x^2}{4} \\right) \\le \\frac{1}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"136\" height=\"45\" />.\nSome simple computations show that this is the same as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/5/d15ab3826555e2554e1520b83ab57cd40b97473e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1 - x)(3 - x - x^2) \\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"187\" height=\"21\" />.\nThis is true because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/a/b0addc9ec9b26b8984517928a8263cba9e5a27f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 &lt; x \\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"79\" height=\"16\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/f/2cff15f3ad09f16b664f8a97b71d66d82aa6516b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-x^2 - x + 3 &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"131\" height=\"18\" /> on the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/3/78378119fcc7e1e8b87d9d7378d502ce679bfc2a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Note\">Note</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Note\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/e/d6ed1c4de01e7f8ff4558009be29235aba384fda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V = \\frac{1}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"54\" height=\"38\" /> is achieved when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/3/34310f2f36ed6d724838edb08788ee62acb33386.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> and all inequalities\nare equalities.  This is the case when all sides except <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/9196e45a2d68e3f48c4f60237064f77b19ddb11e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/0/d200f8253c5adb5e4b97d8dba4467811439aa59a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P = R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"14\" /> are the midpoint of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/5/e05a93b238d96772244b9ccfbcbbd8b9737eab09.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q = P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" /> (in which case the planes\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/3/fb399b42684d8b029a88d9a4620ba9629dc9f527.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC, ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" /> are perpendicular).  In this case, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/a/39ae710e47273544c0ab223494f8dcc423f202e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD = \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"84\" height=\"43\" />,\nand <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/e/d6ed1c4de01e7f8ff4558009be29235aba384fda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V = \\frac{1}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"54\" height=\"38\" /> as can be seen from an easy computation.\n</p><p>[This is an edited version of the solution by jgnr.]\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/2/812a5bf65a623b4fb2fddedb8c99a2b981265d15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{T}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be the set of tetrahedrons with five edges <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/1/8d1d3e9b91fd51e596ff2f380746034a1d58aa60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"27\" height=\"14\" />.\nThis proof will show that there is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/1/3618f570a0994684fbce7be2bc4647eae729dc42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T \\in \\mathcal{T}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" /> with one edge\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/8/31892a6fc3ee94697724ac48f6fe39903de594ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$&gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" /> and such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/d/48d4d22323e016a72b5908cb2c6046243365fcd9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{volume} (T) = \\frac{1}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"132\" height=\"38\" />, and that for\nany <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/2/fd2429ab8956f9967c1b4171199c46e8019ad8fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U \\in \\mathcal{T}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10fed77cf725b1454dd206aae6264b794ff8c5db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U = T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"54\" height=\"14\" /> or there is a finite sequence\nof tetrahedrons <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/7/fc7c2b52c25dc4c4ef86d4b3d97a5f806fea4bcd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T_1, \\dots, T_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"80\" height=\"18\" /> such that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/e/c7e12e61cefcc46ca0ab16632832b3c7ce5d874c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{volume} (U) = \\mathbf{volume} (T_1) &lt; \\dots &lt; \\mathbf{volume} (T_n) = \\mathbf{volume} (T)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"503\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>The statement of the problem is a consequence of these facts.\n</p><p>We begin with two simple propositions.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proposition\">Proposition</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Proposition\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> be a tetrahedron, and consider the transformations which\nrotate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> around <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> while keeping <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/5/f75d6d58b03031bd7cb52a7ca886a8ddf369d21c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" />\nfixed.  We get a set of tetrahedrons, two of which, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/f/57fe35e46017e52065672009599ba7fecfd19a11.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC_1D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"17\" /> and\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/d/b7d21d2a8a2c58fc1485abac5795b8f999e7bce1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC_2D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"17\" /> are shown in the picture below.  The lengths of all sides\nexcept <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/0948661e822f2a953c43a57ac9e40b2734476de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> are constant through this transformation.\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Prob_1967_2_fig2.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/6/6b/Prob_1967_2_fig2.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"377\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>1. Assume that the angles between the planes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6adfd3ad27a2dddeb0af7131d73c33c86991e93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6adfd3ad27a2dddeb0af7131d73c33c86991e93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />\nand <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/0/750b925a66ac92ba5840cf07a578502bc5ed8f4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"47\" height=\"15\" /> are both acute.  If the perpendicular from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/b/70b97eb4ad6e4e7a5a97e2547d9ed6e965960d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> to the\nplane <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6adfd3ad27a2dddeb0af7131d73c33c86991e93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> is larger that the perpendicular from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to the plane\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6adfd3ad27a2dddeb0af7131d73c33c86991e93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> then the volume of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/f/57fe35e46017e52065672009599ba7fecfd19a11.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC_1D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"17\" /> is larger than the volume of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>2. Furthermore, the tetrahedron <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/d/b7d21d2a8a2c58fc1485abac5795b8f999e7bce1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC_2D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"17\" /> obtained when the position of\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/5/b65e6facfe122c182fe14d503aeace2c52a9739d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> is such that the planes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/a/a6adfd3ad27a2dddeb0af7131d73c33c86991e93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/9/3e980ebca0b1841edaddb8157361f2cb5bfed6f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"17\" /> are perpendicular has\nthe maximum volume of all tetrahedrons obtained from rotating\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> around <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>These statements are intuitively clear, since the volume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/d/12d58aa29201da09d8e620f8698e3a37547f6b4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> of the\ntetrahedron <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> is given by\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/1/801ef0651fa8f3f39ba4512284fd899e74646f93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V = \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot ($\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"74\" height=\"38\" />area of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/c/8dc4d8f47c1a090c39f243890c99528bdaf461f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD) \\cdot ($\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"89\" height=\"20\" />height from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c688e52ae9090199d46395644657bbd61f561ec1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>A formal proof is very easy, and I will skip it.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Corollary\">Corollary</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Corollary\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Given a tetrahedron <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, and an edge <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/1/f814fcbba7b623f656f7ce45b87262a9cf562dba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> of it, we can find another\ntetrahedron <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/f/abfe43799d5e541c3d4a2927a7edaf141783cd31.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{volume}(U) &gt; \\mathbf{volume}(T)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"213\" height=\"20\" />,\nwith an edge <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/2/4/a247c76bbca6aa1976c1cfec08e5777759710e03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_1 &gt; e_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" />, and such that all the other edges of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" />\nare equal to the corresponding edges of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/5/4a56be93561739ad72f3b538040740e0a160de69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{unless}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"57\" height=\"14\" /> the\nedge <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/1/f814fcbba7b623f656f7ce45b87262a9cf562dba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> stretches between sides of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> which are perpendicular.\nWhen we chose a bigger <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/a/37a9e7fca70e2dce829d902af2088735306bc1a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"14\" height=\"16\" />, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/f/5ff84ad5d348ff3f6839013468895901d4385a2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_1 &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"17\" /> we can choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/c/a4ca7d98d52e1d5a3d5e9ae5fcf8c743dec85119.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_1 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" />.\nOr, we can choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/a/37a9e7fca70e2dce829d902af2088735306bc1a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"14\" height=\"16\" /> such that it stretches between sides which\nare perpendicular.\n</p><p>(By \"stretches between two sides\" I mean that the end points of the\nedge are the vertices on the two sides which are not common to the\ntwo sides.  In the picture above, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/f/daf153ad55f64c90e096e9eaec639f3e7038d3af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DC_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"38\" height=\"17\" /> stretches between the sides\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/b/3dbe5c769ee80004f44a941ade1dac1ec6887ed8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABD, AC_2B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"103\" height=\"18\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/d/b7d21d2a8a2c58fc1485abac5795b8f999e7bce1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC_2D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"17\" />.)\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Lemma\">Lemma</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Lemma\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Assume we have a tetrahedron <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> with edges <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/0/b7000fbcf873678d6d90f0ac57828428216fd7d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_1, \\dots, e_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" />, such\nthat <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/a/dfa32e480a6294498490694286a83b0558d054e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_2, \\dots, e_6 \\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"107\" height=\"17\" />.  If there is an edge <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/6/866670abbd2fbd82f9a41a634e6dc6c54c8ec2e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_m &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"17\" /> among\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/c/d5c6cbc1fdb4bfadd33d21ee2b8ee995a61d1dd0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_2, \\dots, e_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" /> then there is a tetrahedron <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> with volume bigger\nthan the volume of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, whose edges are equal to those of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />,\nexcept for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/8/7a83b87a9b6bc41b01d3940335473f0ec5ef26da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"13\" />, which is replaced by an edge of size <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h4><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h4>\n<p>Case 1: If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> does not have any sides which are perpendicular, then\nthe existence of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> follows from the corollary.\n</p><p>Case 2: Assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> has exactly two sides which are perpendicular\n(like <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/d/b7d21d2a8a2c58fc1485abac5795b8f999e7bce1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC_2D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"17\" /> in the picture above).  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/4/954666a44e77b922be45d2d260349456c91726e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_2D &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"71\" height=\"17\" /> and it were\nthe only edge <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/7/3c778653546a73cbbe189898f0a1e7474eb53cb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$&lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" />, then all the other edges are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/9196e45a2d68e3f48c4f60237064f77b19ddb11e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" /> (because\nthey were assumed to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/1/8d1d3e9b91fd51e596ff2f380746034a1d58aa60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"27\" height=\"14\" />).  In this case\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/061547cf5a72911311de94598c362d72d76fca37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD, \\triangle AC_2B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"135\" height=\"18\" /> are equilateral with sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/9196e45a2d68e3f48c4f60237064f77b19ddb11e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" />,\nand the planes can not be perpendicular since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/4/954666a44e77b922be45d2d260349456c91726e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_2D &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"71\" height=\"17\" />.  (Indeed,\nan easy computation shows that if we take two equilateral triangles\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/061547cf5a72911311de94598c362d72d76fca37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABD, \\triangle AC_2B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"135\" height=\"18\" /> and place them perpendicular to each\nother, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/c/7ecfd0e9a064e8cd67f8960bf7667bcb64dc541a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CD = \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2} &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"117\" height=\"43\" />.)  So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/8/7a83b87a9b6bc41b01d3940335473f0ec5ef26da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"23\" height=\"13\" /> must be one of\nthe other sides.  Then again, the existence of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> follows from the\ncorollary.  \n</p><p>Case 3: Assume that three sides are perpendicular.\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Prob_1967_2_fig3.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/7/77/Prob_1967_2_fig3.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"389\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>Assume the perpendicular sides are the ones meeting at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, i.e.\neach pair of the planes meeting at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> are perpendicular.  Since\nat least two of the edges <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/7/3e79d02988c6236111144b88d55e4e166daea499.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD, BC, CD \\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"139\" height=\"18\" /> it follows that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/7/307d254157b56050aedc77037f5d24796afd4083.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB, AC, AD &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"136\" height=\"18\" /> (the sides of the right angle in a right triangle\nare less than the hypotenuse).  Just for the sake of notation,\nassume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/3/6a35742141159995cd3a2aa6f760a08b2780c6e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_m = AB &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"109\" height=\"17\" />.  We can apply the corollary, and find a\ntetrahedron <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> with volume bigger than the volume of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, with\nedges equal to those of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, except that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> is replaced by an\nedge <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/9196e45a2d68e3f48c4f60237064f77b19ddb11e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>Now the problem is very easy to prove.  Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2554b6496c3b678897e9b060ef00aa9f0a7d7ece.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$T$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be a tetrahedron\nwith edges <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/0/b7000fbcf873678d6d90f0ac57828428216fd7d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_1, \\dots, e_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"13\" />, such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/a/dfa32e480a6294498490694286a83b0558d054e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_2, \\dots, e_6 \\le 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"107\" height=\"17\" />.\nApply the lemma as many times as necessary (up to five times),\nsuccessively replacing each edge <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/7/3c778653546a73cbbe189898f0a1e7474eb53cb7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$&lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" /> by an edge <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/9196e45a2d68e3f48c4f60237064f77b19ddb11e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" />.  We\nobtain a tetrahedron <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/b/e2bbebb3bd73f1ae5c64098ab0244f739abf7ca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$U$\" width=\"14\" height=\"12\" /> with five edges <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/1/791f2ff592ef7c000b5bc0e094497e5451f31e50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_2, \\dots, f_6 = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" />,\nand one edge <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/0/7c043ac55c0750c14dacc9a43b0fc9546083b62d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_1 = e_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" />.  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/a/37a9e7fca70e2dce829d902af2088735306bc1a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"14\" height=\"16\" /> stretches between two\nperpendicular sides, we are done.  If not, apply the corollary\none more time to obtain a bigger tetrahedron in which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/a/37a9e7fca70e2dce829d902af2088735306bc1a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"14\" height=\"16\" /> is\nreplaced by a larger edge which stretches between two\nperpendicular sides.\n</p><p>We obtain the same result as in the first solution: the largest\ntetrahedron is the one formed by two equilateral triangles with\nsides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/9/9196e45a2d68e3f48c4f60237064f77b19ddb11e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$= 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" />, having one side in common, with the two planes\ncontaining the triangles perpendicular.  An easy calculation\nshows that the edge which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/8/31892a6fc3ee94697724ac48f6fe39903de594ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$&gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"27\" height=\"12\" /> is in fact of length\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/d/44d84cf8889ea171226860bcec5dc9bc95193a1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"26\" height=\"41\" />, and the volume of this tetrahedron\nis <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/b/5db9e227e8177d26de452c2a2faeb6d45cc53d7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>[Solution by pf02, September 2024]\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO\" title=\"1967 IMO\">1967 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1967 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1967 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1967 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1967 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1967 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1967 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1967 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1967_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1967 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017025042\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.067 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.105 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 810/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 895/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 29915/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    7.441      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    7.441      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:12079:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017025042 and revision id 233142. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}